    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1014">

                <pb facs="1521" n="1507" sameAs="frus:frus1944v01/pg1507"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.711/74: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-03-08T17:00:00+01:00">March 8, 1944—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 5:58 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1878. For Dunn<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1014-f1"/> from Phillips.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d1014-f2"/> It would be helpful in dealing with questions involving
                    censorship control in liberated areas if I might be informed whether such
                    censorship should be used solely as a military measure or might be broadened in
                    scope to include such matters as trade information, etc., for use in the post
                    war period. My personal understanding is that it should be used only as a
                    military expedient but I should appreciate receiving the Department’s views.
                    [Phillips.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1014-f1" n="1" xml:id="d1014-f1-content">
                <p>James Clement Dunn, Director of the Office of European Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1014-f2" n="2" xml:id="d1014-f2-content">
                <p>William Phillips, Political Adviser at the Headquarters of the Supreme Allied
                    Commander, London.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
